WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.027 --> 00:00:02.370
- One of my earliest
lessons in money management

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.770
came not from my parents or teachers,

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:05.857
but from a game:

00:00:05.857 --> 00:00:06.930
"Monopoly."

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.490
While my sister went into debt,

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.640
buying every piece of property
she could get her hands on,

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.610
I focused on becoming a railroad tycoon.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.610
Owning all four railroads got you $200

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.620
every time someone landed on one,

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:20.453
not a lot,

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:22.710
but a nice, steady stream of income.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.290
Meanwhile, I kept a lot of cash on hand

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:28.260
and spent as much time
in jail as possible.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.180
This didn't always result in victory,

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:33.600
but it did teach me the
value of an emergency fund.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.330
No matter how many
properties my sister owned,

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:37.860
without any liquidity,

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.720
just one bad roll could throw her

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.390
into a debt spiral she'd
never recover from.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:46.140
- America currently ranks
14th amongst wealthy nations

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.490
in financial literacy.

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.770
Roughly 2/3 of young
adults don't understand

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.180
basic financial concepts,

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.910
like inflation and risk diversification.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:54.840
Why?

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.010
Most schools don't have

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.020
dedicated personal financial courses.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.090
And only about half of parents say

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.940
they feel comfortable discussing
money with their kids.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.590
- But there is one place young people

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.480
have more opportunities than ever

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.430
to learn financial concepts:

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:09.337
games.

00:01:09.337 --> 00:01:12.870
"Monopoly" is far from a
perfect real estate simulator,

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:16.260
but it is the first place I
ever heard the words mortgage,

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:17.730
deed, and rent.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.560
Other board games from our childhood

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.840
like "Pay Day" and the "Game of Life"

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.340
taught us about budgets,

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.410
salaries, taxes, and retirement,

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.290
and we didn't even know
we were learning anything.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.300
We were just having fun.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.240
- Though these games are
old-fashioned by today's standards,

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.520
you could argue that
money is more prevalent

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.290
than ever in game design.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.900
There are a lot of financial
lessons being learned

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.420
and habits being formed in modern games,

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:43.950
and they're not all good ones.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:46.950
- But educators are starting
to see the potential in gaming

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.920
to teach young people how to
manage their money better,

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.080
and hopefully, close that
financial literacy gap.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.663
(bright music)

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.200
Back when "Super Mario
Brothers" first came out,

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.460
in 1985,

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.710
video game money was very simple.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:08.760
You collect coins,

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.020
and when you get to a hundred,

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.870
they're automatically
traded in for an extra life.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.390
Since there was nothing
else you could do with them,

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.250
there were no decisions to make.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.860
Today, virtually every game asks you

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.930
how you wanna spend your money:

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.270
do you wanna buy some more health potions

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:25.920
or upgrade your armor?

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.510
Increase your spell slots

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.790
or get a cute hat for your cat?

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.410
- These decisions can teach kids

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:34.200
the importance of saving
up for a desired purchase

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.320
and a rudimentary understanding
of opportunity costs:

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.560
the concept that every purchase
you make comes at the cost

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.440
of what you could have
spent that money on instead.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.690
And though that money may be imaginary,

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:47.580
if you've spent hundreds of hours

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.200
grinding in "World of Warcraft,"

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.100
like I have,

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.830
you take those decisions very seriously.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:53.700
- Grinding?

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:56.820
- Yeah, it means doing
mundane, repetitive tasks

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.886
for hours on end to earn money.

00:02:58.886 --> 00:03:01.590
- I guess that's kind of a life lesson?

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:02.640
Since the late '80s,

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:06.660
simulation games have taken
game economics to a new level,

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:09.240
allowing players to build
and manage everything

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.220
from farms and amusement parks

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.920
to cities and entire civilizations.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.560
Budgeting is a huge part of these games,

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.980
requiring players to balance
income and expenditures

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:21.870
while leaving enough leftover

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:23.910
to reinvest in expansion

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.640
and save up for the unexpected disaster.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.020
- Interestingly enough,

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.170
one of the most fiscally
advanced games of recent years

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.247
is also one of the cutest:

00:03:33.247 --> 00:03:35.010
"Animal Crossing: New Horizons"

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.830
places you on a picturesque
little island populated

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:39.420
by adorable talking animals.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.670
And one of the first
ones you meet informs you

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:43.230
that you owe him money.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:44.063
That's right.

00:03:44.063 --> 00:03:45.330
You start the game with a mortgage

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.820
and have to immediately
start hustling to pay it off

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:49.890
by harvesting flora and fauna

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.770
and selling it for a
currency called bells.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:54.630
- The game also contains an example

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:57.630
of the economic principle
known as arbitrage,

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:59.640
in that, you can sell your resources

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.230
of other people's islands

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:02.340
where they're more rare,

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.530
and therefore, fetch higher price.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:08.190
There's even a "stalk market"
where prices fluctuate,

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:11.760
and you have to time your
selling for maximum profit.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.200
Though, in the real world,

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.020
trying to time buying and
selling on the stock market

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:17.850
is a dangerous practice.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.100
Not recommended for novice players.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.220
- But video games don't
just use imaginary money,

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:24.150
real dollars,

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:25.230
and lots of them,

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.660
are being spent on in-game merch,

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.550
whether that's within the game itself

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:31.860
or on one of the many
external marketplaces

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.710
for rare items that have sprung up online.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:37.290
A lot of these digital
goods aren't even useful,

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.760
they're purely superficial,

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:41.190
like "Fortnite" skins and emotes.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:43.050
This may teach the unfortunate lesson

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:45.090
that money's primary purpose

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:47.880
is to acquire external symbols of status.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:48.720
- Conversely,

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:51.900
some kids are actually
making money within games.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.397
One of the most popular
games in the world right now,

00:04:54.397 --> 00:04:57.480
"Roblox," is actually more
of a digital marketplace

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.450
where anyone can design
their own experience

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:02.400
and charge others to play it.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.100
Though they advertise
lucrative earning potential,

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:09.090
the company actually takes
over 75% of all sales,

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:12.330
far more than any other
game distribution platform.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:14.850
- According to a report
by "People Make Games,"

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:17.760
these Robux lose a lot of their value

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:19.590
when they're converted into real dollars,

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:21.030
which encourages creators

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:23.700
to spend their earnings within the game.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.260
The authors of the report compare it

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:28.950
to the mining camp policy of
paying workers in a currency

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:31.380
that can only be spent
at the company store,

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:34.380
a practice that is now illegal IRL.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.210
Being exploited for one's labor

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.490
by a giant corporation could be seen

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:39.930
as a valuable money lesson,

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.060
but only if a parent or other adult

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:44.790
is willing to help you make
sense of what's going on.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:46.260
- Most of the games we've talked about

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:49.080
impart financial lessons as a side effect

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.780
but financial educators are
starting to design games

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:53.370
with the explicit purpose

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:55.170
of teaching young people about money.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:56.790
- Our friends over at NOVA Labs

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:58.770
recently released a few of their own.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:00.690
Here to tell you more
about the Financial Lab

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:02.220
is Yanely Espinal.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:03.357
- Rather than overloading players

00:06:03.357 --> 00:06:06.510
with a bunch of math and terminology,

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:09.450
NOVA's games focus on
the psychology of money,

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:11.820
specifically the intention gap:

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:14.310
the difference between
what we know we should do

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:17.070
and what we actually end up doing.

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:19.080
They try to help players identify

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.060
and overcome the mental biases

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.550
that lead to common money mistakes,

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:27.300
a field often referred to
as behavioral economics.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:30.390
Our first game focuses
on opportunity costs,

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:33.780
reminding players that when
judging the value of a purchase,

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:35.940
you must incorporate the loss

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:38.640
of what you won't be able
to spend that money on

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:40.140
in the future.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:43.050
The second game deals
with mental accounting:

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:46.500
the strategy of dividing
up your income and expenses

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:48.450
into different categories.

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:52.260
Mental accounting can be
helpful when designing a budget,

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:53.970
but it can also blind you

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:56.850
to the fact that money is fungible.

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:58.800
And the third game teaches you

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.340
that compound interest can
be both your best friend

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:04.290
and your worst enemy,

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:07.440
depending on which side of
the balance sheet it's on.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.450
Playing all three games

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:11.760
is a great way to
practice making decisions

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:15.270
about how to spend your
money in the short, medium,

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:16.980
and long-terms.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:18.120
And I'm sure you'd agree,

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.760
we could all use a little practice.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:22.710
- As a regular viewer of "Two Cents,"

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:24.390
I'm sure you'll have no trouble

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:26.280
acing the NOVA Financial Lab.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:29.100
Although I did have to do
the last game a couple times

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:30.330
to get the top trophy.

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:31.530
- Which is good.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:33.450
Part of the teaching power of games

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:35.370
is that they're repeatable.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:37.230
From puppies play fighting

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:39.390
to haggling over Park Place,

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:42.360
games allow us to practice
important life skills

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:46.260
and make mistakes without
serious life consequences.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:47.760
- [Both] And that's two cents.

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:50.220
- [Julia] Thanks to our patrons

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:52.950
for keeping "Two Cents"
financially healthy.

